  <section class="rm-index not-content" aria-label="All topics on this roadmap">
    <h2 class="rm-index-title">All topics</h2>
    <p class="rm-index-lede">Every node on the roadmap above, as text. Each links to the page section that covers it.</p>
    <ul class="rm-index-list">
      <li class="rm-index-group">
        <h3 class="rm-index-heading"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-distributed-computing-fundamentals/">Distributed Computing Fundamentals</a></h3>
        <p class="rm-index-desc">The core building blocks of how Spark distributes work across a cluster.</p>
        <ul class="rm-index-subs">
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-distributed-computing-fundamentals/#driver-node-responsibilities">Driver node responsibilities</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">The process that runs main(), builds the DAG, negotiates resources with the cluster manager, and schedules tasks onto executors.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-distributed-computing-fundamentals/#worker-node-architecture">Worker node architecture</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Physical or virtual machines that host executor processes, providing the CPU, memory, and disk resources tasks run on.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-distributed-computing-fundamentals/#executor-processes-and-task-slots">Executor processes and task slots</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">JVM processes launched per application on worker nodes that run tasks in parallel across configurable core-based slots and cache data in memory.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-distributed-computing-fundamentals/#cluster-manager-concepts">Cluster manager concepts (YARN, Mesos, Standalone, Kubernetes)</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Pluggable systems that allocate cluster resources and launch executors on Spark's behalf.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-distributed-computing-fundamentals/#spark-master-and-worker-roles">Spark master and worker roles</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">In Standalone mode, the master arbitrates resources across applications while workers register with it and spawn executors on request.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-distributed-computing-fundamentals/#resource-allocation-strategies">Resource allocation strategies</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Static allocation reserves a fixed set of executors up front, while dynamic and per-job configuration trade flexibility for predictability.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-distributed-computing-fundamentals/#dynamic-resource-allocation">Dynamic resource allocation</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Lets Spark scale executors up and down at runtime based on pending task backlog, releasing idle capacity back to the cluster.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-distributed-computing-fundamentals/#node-heartbeats-and-health-checks">Node heartbeats and health checks</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Periodic signals executors send the driver to report liveness and block status; missed heartbeats trigger executor loss handling.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li class="rm-index-group">
        <h3 class="rm-index-heading"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-execution-lifecycle/">Cluster Architecture &amp; Execution Lifecycle</a></h3>
        <p class="rm-index-desc">How a Spark application is broken down, scheduled, and executed.</p>
        <ul class="rm-index-subs">
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-execution-lifecycle/#job-stage-and-task-hierarchy">Job, stage, and task hierarchy</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">An action triggers a job, which the DAG scheduler splits into stages at shuffle boundaries, each stage made up of parallel tasks.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-execution-lifecycle/#dag-scheduler-flow">DAG scheduler flow</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Translates the logical execution plan into a physical DAG of stages, tracking dependencies and resubmitting failed stages as needed.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-execution-lifecycle/#narrow-versus-wide-dependencies">Narrow versus wide dependencies</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Narrow dependencies (map, filter) require no shuffle and can pipeline within a stage; wide dependencies (groupBy, join) force a shuffle and new stage.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-execution-lifecycle/#task-scheduling-and-parallelism">Task scheduling and parallelism</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">The task scheduler assigns tasks to executor cores based on locality preferences, queueing tasks when slots aren't yet available.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-execution-lifecycle/#data-locality-awareness">Data locality awareness</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Prefers scheduling tasks on nodes that already hold the relevant data (PROCESS_LOCAL to NO_PREF/ANY) to minimize network I/O.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-execution-lifecycle/#threading-models-within-executors">Threading models within executors</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Executors run multiple tasks concurrently as threads within a single JVM, sharing memory but isolated per-task via thread-local state.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-execution-lifecycle/#shuffle-phases-and-mechanics">Shuffle phases and mechanics</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Splits into a map (write) side that partitions and spills output, and a reduce (read) side that fetches and merges shuffle blocks.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-execution-lifecycle/#data-serialization-during-shuffle">Data serialization during shuffle</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Data written to shuffle files must be serialized (Java or Kryo) and deserialized on read, making format choice a major performance lever.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li class="rm-index-group">
        <h3 class="rm-index-heading"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-core-fundamentals/">Spark Core Fundamentals</a></h3>
        <p class="rm-index-desc">Getting a Spark application up, configured, and observable.</p>
        <ul class="rm-index-subs">
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-core-fundamentals/#sparksession-sparkcontext-and-legacy-sqlcontexthivecontext">SparkSession, SparkContext, and legacy SQLContext/HiveContext</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">SparkSession is the unified entry point since 2.0, wrapping the lower-level SparkContext and superseding the legacy SQLContext/HiveContext APIs.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-core-fundamentals/#spark-submit-flags-and-master-url-formats">spark-submit flags and master URL formats</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">The CLI for launching applications — flags for driver/executor memory and cores, deploy mode, and master URLs (local, yarn, k8s://, spark://).</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-core-fundamentals/#spark-configuration-and-environment-variables">Spark configuration and environment variables</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Settings layered via SparkConf, spark-defaults.conf, spark-submit flags, and environment variables like SPARK_HOME and JAVA_HOME, in increasing precedence.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-core-fundamentals/#logging-level-configuration">Logging level configuration</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Controlled via log4j2 properties files to tune verbosity per package, separating driver and executor log output for debugging.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-core-fundamentals/#spark-ui-monitoring-basics">Spark UI monitoring basics</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">The web UI (default port 4040) exposing Jobs, Stages, Storage, Environment, Executors, and SQL tabs for live application inspection.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-core-fundamentals/#spark-connect-client-server-architecture">Spark Connect client-server architecture</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Decouples client applications from the cluster via a gRPC protocol, letting thin clients submit DataFrame plans without a local Spark install.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li class="rm-index-group">
        <h3 class="rm-index-heading"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-rdd-api/">Resilient Distributed Datasets (RDDs)</a></h3>
        <p class="rm-index-desc">The low-level, fault-tolerant distributed collection API underlying all of Spark.</p>
        <ul class="rm-index-subs">
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-rdd-api/#rdd-creation-methods">RDD creation methods</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">RDDs are created by parallelizing a driver-side collection, reading external storage, or transforming an existing RDD.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-rdd-api/#transformation-operations">Transformation operations</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Lazy operations like map, filter, and flatMap that return a new RDD without triggering computation until an action runs.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-rdd-api/#action-operations">Action operations</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Operations like collect, count, and reduce that trigger execution of the RDD's lineage and return a result to the driver.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-rdd-api/#lazy-evaluation-model">Lazy evaluation model</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Transformations build up a lineage graph without executing; Spark only computes when an action forces evaluation, enabling optimization.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-rdd-api/#dependency-graphs-and-lineage">Dependency graphs and lineage</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Each RDD tracks its parent RDDs and the transformation that produced it, forming the lineage graph used for fault recovery.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-rdd-api/#persistence-and-storage-levels">Persistence and storage levels</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">cache()/persist() with storage levels (MEMORY_ONLY, MEMORY_AND_DISK, off-heap, replicated) that trade memory pressure against recomputation cost.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-rdd-api/#rdd-checkpointing-and-lineage-truncation">RDD checkpointing and lineage truncation</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Writes an RDD to reliable storage and truncates its lineage, preventing stack overflows and excessive recomputation on very long chains.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-rdd-api/#recovering-failed-tasks-and-executor-loss-mitigation">Recovering failed tasks and executor loss mitigation</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Lost partitions are recomputed from lineage on healthy executors; a lost executor triggers reassignment of its in-flight and cached tasks.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-rdd-api/#broadcast-variables">Broadcast variables</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Read-only variables cached once per executor rather than shipped with every task, used to efficiently share lookup tables or join keys.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-rdd-api/#accumulator-usage-patterns">Accumulator usage patterns</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Write-only variables that aggregate values across tasks back to the driver, commonly used for counters and debugging metrics.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-rdd-api/#custom-partitioning-hash-and-range">Custom partitioning (hash and range)</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">HashPartitioner distributes keys by hash for even spread; RangePartitioner sorts keys into ordered ranges, both usable via custom subclasses.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li class="rm-index-group">
        <h3 class="rm-index-heading"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-dataframe-dataset-apis/">DataFrame &amp; Dataset APIs</a></h3>
        <p class="rm-index-desc">The structured, higher-level APIs used for most modern Spark development.</p>
        <ul class="rm-index-subs">
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-dataframe-dataset-apis/#dataframe-creation-patterns">DataFrame creation patterns</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">DataFrames are built from RDDs, structured files, JDBC sources, Hive tables, or in-memory collections via createDataFrame.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-dataframe-dataset-apis/#column-expressions-and-operations">Column expressions and operations</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">The Column API (col(), expr(), withColumn) for building typed transformations and arithmetic, string, and conditional expressions.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-dataframe-dataset-apis/#row-manipulation-functions">Row manipulation functions</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Functions like withColumn, drop, select, and struct-building operations for reshaping individual Row values within a DataFrame.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-dataframe-dataset-apis/#schema-definition-and-inference">Schema definition and inference</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Schemas can be inferred from data (at a scan cost) or defined explicitly via StructType/StructField for stability and performance.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-dataframe-dataset-apis/#null-handling-strategies">Null handling strategies</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">na.drop, na.fill, coalesce, and isNull/isNotNull predicates for detecting and resolving missing values in a DataFrame.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-dataframe-dataset-apis/#column-type-casting">Column type casting</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Explicit type conversion via cast() or astype(), important for avoiding silent precision loss or failed comparisons across mismatched types.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-dataframe-dataset-apis/#dataset-api-and-type-safety">Dataset API and type safety (Scala/Java)</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Scala/Java-only typed API layered over DataFrames, catching schema mismatches at compile time instead of at runtime.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-dataframe-dataset-apis/#encoders-and-compile-time-checks">Encoders and compile-time checks</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Encoders convert between JVM objects and Spark's internal binary row format, enabling Dataset type safety and efficient serialization.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-dataframe-dataset-apis/#filtering-sorting-and-selection-logic">Filtering, sorting, and selection logic</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">filter/where, orderBy/sort, and select/selectExpr — the core relational operators for narrowing and reordering DataFrame rows and columns.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-dataframe-dataset-apis/#pandas-api-on-spark">Pandas API on Spark (formerly Koalas)</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">A pandas-compatible API (formerly Koalas) that runs on distributed Spark DataFrames, easing migration of single-node pandas code.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li class="rm-index-group">
        <h3 class="rm-index-heading"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-window-functions/">Window Functions</a></h3>
        <p class="rm-index-desc">Ranking, aggregate, and analytic functions computed over row windows.</p>
        <ul class="rm-index-subs">
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-window-functions/#window-specification-definitions">Window specification definitions</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">WindowSpec defines the partitioning, ordering, and frame boundaries a window function is evaluated over, built via Window.partitionBy/orderBy.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-window-functions/#ranking-functions">Ranking functions (rank, dense_rank, row_number)</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">rank() leaves gaps after ties, dense_rank() doesn't, and row_number() assigns a strictly unique sequential number within each partition.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-window-functions/#aggregate-window-functions">Aggregate window functions</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Standard aggregates like sum, avg, and max computed over a window frame instead of collapsing rows, preserving row-level granularity.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-window-functions/#lead-and-lag-operations">Lead and lag operations</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Access a column's value from a following (lead) or preceding (lag) row within the same partition, for period-over-period comparisons.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-window-functions/#range-versus-row-frames">Range versus row frames</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">ROWS frames count physical rows before/after the current row; RANGE frames include all rows with the same order-by value.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-window-functions/#partitioning-within-windows">Partitioning within windows</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">partitionBy splits rows into independent groups the window function is computed separately within, analogous to GROUP BY without collapsing rows.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-window-functions/#cumulative-and-running-calculations">Cumulative and running calculations</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Running totals and moving averages built with an unbounded preceding-to-current-row frame combined with sum/avg window functions.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li class="rm-index-group">
        <h3 class="rm-index-heading"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-joins/">Joins &amp; Relational Operations</a></h3>
        <p class="rm-index-desc">How Spark combines datasets, and the strategies it picks between.</p>
        <ul class="rm-index-subs">
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-joins/#inner-outer-semi-and-anti-joins">Inner, outer, semi, and anti joins</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Inner keeps matching rows only; outer variants keep unmatched rows from one or both sides; semi/anti filter one side by match existence without duplicating columns.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-joins/#cross-joins-and-cartesian-products">Cross joins and Cartesian products</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Every row of one DataFrame paired with every row of the other; expensive and usually accidental unless explicitly required.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-joins/#shuffle-hash-join">Shuffle hash join</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Both sides are shuffled and hash-partitioned by join key, then joined by building an in-memory hash table on the smaller partition.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-joins/#sort-merge-join">Sort-merge join</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Both sides are shuffled and sorted by join key, then merged in a single linear pass — Spark SQL's default strategy for large-large joins.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-joins/#broadcast-hash-join">Broadcast hash join</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Ships an entire small table to every executor to avoid shuffling the large table, controlled by spark.sql.autoBroadcastJoinThreshold.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-joins/#join-strategy-selection-and-hints">Join strategy selection and hints</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">The optimizer picks a join strategy from size estimates and config thresholds, overridable with hints like broadcast(), merge(), or shuffle_hash().</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-joins/#handling-data-skew-in-joins">Handling data skew in joins</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Skewed key distributions overload a few tasks; mitigated with salting, AQE's automatic skew join optimization, or isolating hot keys.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li class="rm-index-group">
        <h3 class="rm-index-heading"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-advanced-transformations/">Advanced RDD &amp; DataFrame Transformations</a></h3>
        <p class="rm-index-desc">Less common but important transformation patterns.</p>
        <ul class="rm-index-subs">
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-advanced-transformations/#mappartitions-and-mappartitionswithindex">mapPartitions and mapPartitionsWithIndex</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Apply a function once per partition rather than per row, amortizing expensive setup (e.g. connection creation) across all rows in that partition.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-advanced-transformations/#filter-and-predicate-pushdown">Filter and predicate pushdown</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Filter conditions pushed down into the data source scan (e.g. Parquet row groups) so unmatched data is never read into Spark at all.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-advanced-transformations/#set-based-operations">Set-based operations (union, intersect, except)</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">union combines rows from two DataFrames with matching schemas; intersect keeps common rows; except returns rows in one but not the other.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-advanced-transformations/#pivoting-and-unpivoting-data">Pivoting and unpivoting data</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">pivot() turns distinct row values into columns for cross-tab style aggregation; the reverse (unpivot/stack) turns columns back into rows.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-advanced-transformations/#sampling-and-data-splitting">Sampling and data splitting</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">sample() draws a random fraction of rows with or without replacement; randomSplit() partitions a DataFrame into weighted subsets (e.g. train/test).</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-advanced-transformations/#exploding-and-flattening-nestedarray-structures">Exploding and flattening nested/array structures</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">explode()/explode_outer() turn array or map column elements into separate rows; flattening nested structs unpacks them into top-level columns.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li class="rm-index-group">
        <h3 class="rm-index-heading"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-udfs/">User Defined Functions (UDFs)</a></h3>
        <p class="rm-index-desc">Extending Spark SQL with custom logic, and its performance implications.</p>
        <ul class="rm-index-subs">
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-udfs/#scalar-udf-implementation">Scalar UDF implementation</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Row-at-a-time custom functions registered via udf() or spark.udf.register, applied to columns like any built-in Spark SQL function.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-udfs/#user-defined-aggregate-functions-udafs">User-defined aggregate functions (UDAFs)</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Custom aggregations implemented via the Aggregator API (typed) or legacy UserDefinedAggregateFunction, extending groupBy beyond built-in aggregates.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-udfs/#pandas-vectorized-udfs">Pandas (vectorized) UDFs</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Operate on pandas Series/DataFrame batches instead of single rows, using Arrow to avoid per-row Python serialization overhead.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-udfs/#arrow-based-data-transfer-for-udfs">Arrow-based data transfer for UDFs</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Apache Arrow's columnar in-memory format moves batches between the JVM and Python process for vectorized UDFs, cutting serialization cost sharply.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-udfs/#python-versus-jvm-execution-overhead">Python versus JVM execution overhead</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Python UDFs pay a serialization and process-boundary tax per row that native Scala/Java or Pandas UDFs largely avoid.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-udfs/#built-in-functions-versus-custom-udfs">Built-in functions versus custom UDFs (performance tradeoffs)</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Built-in functions run inside Catalyst's optimized, whole-stage-codegen path; UDFs are opaque black boxes the optimizer can't see into or push down through.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-udfs/#higher-order-functions-for-arraymap-columns">Higher-order functions for array/map columns</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">transform, filter, exists, and aggregate operate on array/map columns directly in SQL, avoiding a UDF or explode/collect round-trip.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-udfs/#dependency-isolation-for-udf-environments">Dependency isolation for UDF environments</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Python UDF environments are isolated via virtualenv/conda archives or container images shipped with the job to avoid version conflicts across executors.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li class="rm-index-group">
        <h3 class="rm-index-heading"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-sql-catalyst-optimizer/">Spark SQL Engine &amp; Catalyst Optimizer</a></h3>
        <p class="rm-index-desc">The query planner and optimizer at the heart of Spark SQL.</p>
        <ul class="rm-index-subs">
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-sql-catalyst-optimizer/#logical-plan-analysis-and-validation">Logical plan analysis and validation</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">The Analyzer resolves column and table references against the catalog and checks types, turning an unresolved plan into a validated logical plan.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-sql-catalyst-optimizer/#rule-based-logical-plan-optimization">Rule-based logical plan optimization</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Catalyst applies a fixed set of rules — predicate pushdown, constant folding, column pruning — to rewrite the logical plan into an equivalent, cheaper one.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-sql-catalyst-optimizer/#physical-plan-generation-and-selection">Physical plan generation and selection</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">The optimized logical plan is turned into one or more physical plans (e.g. join strategies), and the planner picks the cheapest by cost.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-sql-catalyst-optimizer/#cost-based-optimization-cbo">Cost-based optimization (CBO)</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Uses collected table and column statistics to estimate operator costs and choose better join orders and strategies than rule-based heuristics alone.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-sql-catalyst-optimizer/#whole-stage-code-generation">Whole-stage code generation</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Fuses multiple physical operators into a single generated Java function per stage, eliminating virtual function call overhead row by row.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-sql-catalyst-optimizer/#vectorized-execution">Vectorized execution</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Processes columnar batches of rows at once (e.g. for Parquet scans) instead of row-at-a-time, exploiting CPU cache and SIMD better.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-sql-catalyst-optimizer/#adaptive-query-execution-aqe">Adaptive Query Execution (AQE)</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Re-optimizes the physical plan at runtime using actual shuffle statistics — switching join strategies, coalescing partitions, and handling skew after seeing real data sizes.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-sql-catalyst-optimizer/#dynamic-partition-coalescing">Dynamic partition coalescing</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">AQE merges small post-shuffle partitions into larger ones at runtime, avoiding the overhead of many tiny tasks from a static partition count.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-sql-catalyst-optimizer/#dynamic-join-strategy-switching">Dynamic join strategy switching</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">AQE can downgrade a sort-merge join to a broadcast join at runtime once actual shuffle output size is known to be small enough.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-sql-catalyst-optimizer/#skew-join-optimization">Skew join optimization</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">AQE detects disproportionately large shuffle partitions and splits them into smaller sub-partitions joined separately, avoiding a single straggler task.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-sql-catalyst-optimizer/#plan-caching">Plan caching</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Spark caches parsed and analyzed logical plans for repeated query shapes to avoid re-running the parser and analyzer on every execution.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-sql-catalyst-optimizer/#reading-and-interpreting-explain-plans">Reading and interpreting explain plans</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">explain() and its formatted/cost variants show parsed, analyzed, optimized, and physical plans — essential for diagnosing join strategy and shuffle behavior.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-sql-catalyst-optimizer/#spark-thrift-server">Spark Thrift Server (JDBC/ODBC access for BI tools)</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">A long-running JDBC/ODBC server exposing Spark SQL to BI tools like Tableau, sharing a SparkContext across concurrent client connections.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li class="rm-index-group">
        <h3 class="rm-index-heading"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-file-formats-storage/">File Formats &amp; Storage Optimization</a></h3>
        <p class="rm-index-desc">Choosing and tuning the on-disk formats Spark reads and writes.</p>
        <ul class="rm-index-subs">
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/formats/parquet/">Parquet storage format and optimization</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Columnar, self-describing binary format with per-column encoding and stats — Spark's default and best-optimized format for analytical scans.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-file-formats-storage/#orc-file-structure-and-benefits">ORC file structure and benefits</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Columnar format with built-in indexes and lightweight compression, popular in the Hive ecosystem, with strong predicate pushdown support.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/formats/avro/">Avro serialization and schema</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Row-based format with schema embedded in every file, favored for streaming and schema-registry-driven pipelines over columnar analytics.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-file-formats-storage/#schema-evolution-across-formats">Schema evolution across formats</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Parquet and Avro support adding/removing/renaming columns across file versions differently — Avro via writer/reader schema resolution, Parquet via schema merging.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/formats/parquet/">Columnar projection and column pruning</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Only the columns referenced by the query are read off disk, skipping unused column chunks entirely in columnar formats like Parquet and ORC.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/formats/parquet/">Dictionary encoding mechanics</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Low-cardinality columns are stored as small integer codes plus a lookup dictionary, shrinking file size and speeding up filters and joins.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/formats/parquet/">Compression codec selection (Snappy, Zstd, GZIP)</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Snappy favors decompression speed for interactive queries; Zstd offers better ratios at moderate CPU cost; GZIP maximizes compression at the cost of speed.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-file-formats-storage/#small-file-problem-and-compaction-strategies">Small file problem and compaction strategies</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Many tiny files overload the driver's file-listing and task-scheduling overhead; fixed by coalescing/repartitioning writes or periodic compaction jobs.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/formats/parquet/">Predicate and partition pushdown</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Filters are pushed into the file scan (row-group/stripe skipping) and into partition directory pruning so entire files or partitions are skipped unread.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-file-formats-storage/#metadata-and-footer-caching-strategies">Metadata and footer caching strategies</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Parquet/ORC footers holding schema and column statistics are cached across tasks to avoid re-reading them for every file on every query.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li class="rm-index-group">
        <h3 class="rm-index-heading"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-cloud-db-connectors/">Cloud Storage &amp; Database Connectors</a></h3>
        <p class="rm-index-desc">Reading from and writing to object stores and relational databases.</p>
        <ul class="rm-index-subs">
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-cloud-db-connectors/#s3-gcs-and-adls-integration">S3, GCS, and ADLS integration</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Cloud object stores are accessed through Hadoop-compatible filesystem connectors (S3A, GCS connector, ABFS) using s3a://, gs://, and abfss:// URI schemes.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-cloud-db-connectors/#s3a-implementation-and-file-path-patterns">S3A implementation and file path patterns</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">The S3A connector emulates a filesystem over S3's flat object namespace, with tunable settings for multipart upload, committers, and consistency.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-cloud-db-connectors/#partitioned-directory-reading">Partitioned directory reading</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Reading Hive-style partitioned paths (key=value directories) lets Spark infer partition columns and prune entire directories via filter pushdown.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-cloud-db-connectors/#jdbc-drivers-and-connection-pooling">JDBC drivers and connection pooling</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">spark.read.jdbc requires a driver JAR on the classpath and benefits from numPartitions/fetchsize tuning; connections aren't pooled across tasks by default.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-cloud-db-connectors/#predicate-pushdown-for-database-queries">Predicate pushdown for database queries</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Supported filter predicates are translated into the source database's WHERE clause so filtering happens in the database, not after the full table lands in Spark.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-cloud-db-connectors/#partitioned-database-reads-and-write-batching">Partitioned database reads and write batching</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Parallel JDBC reads split a table by a partition column/bounds into concurrent range queries; writes use batchsize to control rows per JDBC batch insert.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-cloud-db-connectors/#credential-and-secrets-management-for-connectors">Credential and secrets management for connectors</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Cloud and DB credentials are supplied via IAM roles/instance profiles, secret managers, or Hadoop Credential Providers rather than hardcoded in configs.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-cloud-db-connectors/#kafka-and-rest-apis-as-batch-data-sources">Kafka and REST APIs as batch data sources</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Kafka topics can be read as a bounded batch (not just a stream) via startingOffsets/endingOffsets; REST APIs are pulled via custom or third-party connectors.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-cloud-db-connectors/#hadoop-file-system-hdfs-interaction">Hadoop file system (HDFS) interaction</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Spark reads/writes HDFS natively via the hdfs:// scheme, inheriting data locality benefits and replication from the underlying Hadoop cluster.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </li>
