        <ul class="rm-index-subs">
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/formats/delta/">Delta Lake ACID transactions and transaction log</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Delta's _delta_log records every commit as an ordered JSON/Parquet checkpoint sequence, giving ACID guarantees and optimistic concurrency over plain object storage.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/formats/iceberg/">Apache Iceberg architecture and partition evolution</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Iceberg tracks table state via a metadata/manifest hierarchy independent of physical layout, letting partition schemes change without rewriting existing data.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/formats/hudi/">Apache Hudi upsert and incremental processing semantics</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Hudi's Copy-on-Write and Merge-on-Read table types support row-level upserts/deletes and expose changed records for efficient incremental pipelines.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-lakehouse-formats/#time-travel-query-capabilities">Time travel query capabilities</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Querying a table as of a past version or timestamp, using the transaction log/snapshot history that Delta, Iceberg, and Hudi each maintain.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-lakehouse-formats/#schema-evolution-strategies">Schema evolution strategies</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Adding, renaming, or widening columns safely on existing tables, with format-specific rules for what's automatic versus what requires explicit migration.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-lakehouse-formats/#merge-into-syntax-and-upsert-patterns">MERGE INTO syntax and upsert patterns</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">SQL MERGE INTO expresses conditional insert/update/delete logic against a target table in one atomic statement — the standard upsert pattern on lakehouse tables.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-lakehouse-formats/#table-maintenance">Table maintenance (compaction, vacuum, Z-ordering/optimize)</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Compaction merges small files, vacuum removes stale unreferenced files past a retention window, and Z-ordering/OPTIMIZE co-locates related data for faster filtering.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li class="rm-index-group">
        <h3 class="rm-index-heading"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-structured-streaming/">Structured Streaming</a></h3>
        <p class="rm-index-desc">Processing unbounded data with the same DataFrame API as batch.</p>
        <ul class="rm-index-subs">
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-structured-streaming/#micro-batch-processing-model">Micro-batch processing model</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Structured Streaming's default engine treats a stream as a sequence of small batch jobs, achieving latencies in the ~100ms range with strong exactly-once guarantees.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-structured-streaming/#continuous-low-latency-processing-mode">Continuous (low-latency) processing mode</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">An experimental engine that processes records as they arrive rather than in micro-batches, targeting ~1ms latency at the cost of weaker guarantees and API support.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-structured-streaming/#streaming-sources-and-sinks">Streaming sources and sinks</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Sources (Kafka, files, sockets) and sinks (files, Kafka, console, foreach, memory) plug into the same DataFrame API used for batch.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-structured-streaming/#kafka-source-integration-and-offset-management">Kafka source integration and offset management</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Reads Kafka topics as a DataFrame of key/value/offset columns; offsets are tracked in the checkpoint rather than committed back to Kafka by default.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-structured-streaming/#event-time-watermarking">Event-time watermarking</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Declares how late data is allowed to arrive relative to event time, letting Spark bound state size by dropping data older than the watermark.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-structured-streaming/#stateful-stream-processing-and-state-stores">Stateful stream processing and state stores</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Operations like aggregations and mapGroupsWithState keep running state in a versioned, checkpointed state store (RocksDB or in-memory) across micro-batches.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-structured-streaming/#late-data-handling">Late data handling</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Records arriving after the watermark threshold for their event-time window are dropped from windowed aggregations to keep state bounded.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-structured-streaming/#output-modes">Output modes (append, update, complete)</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Append emits only new rows since the last trigger, update emits changed rows, and complete re-emits the entire result table each trigger.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-structured-streaming/#checkpointing-and-fault-recovery">Checkpointing and fault recovery</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Persists offsets and operator state to durable storage so a failed streaming query can resume exactly where it left off.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-structured-streaming/#stream-stream-and-stream-static-joins">Stream-stream and stream-static joins</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Stream-static joins re-scan the static side each micro-batch; stream-stream joins require watermarks on both sides to bound buffered unmatched state.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </li>
