        <ul class="rm-index-subs">
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-performance-tuning/#execution-memory-versus-storage-memory">Execution memory versus storage memory (unified memory model)</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">The unified memory manager lets execution (shuffles, joins, sorts) and storage (cached data) share one region, with execution able to evict cached blocks under pressure.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-performance-tuning/#garbage-collection-tuning">Garbage collection tuning (G1GC vs. Parallel GC)</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">G1GC targets predictable pause times on large heaps and is the modern default; Parallel GC maximizes throughput at the cost of longer stop-the-world pauses.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-performance-tuning/#off-heap-memory-allocation">Off-heap memory allocation</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">spark.memory.offHeap moves some data storage outside the JVM heap, reducing GC pressure at the cost of manual memory management.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-performance-tuning/#memory-overhead-limits-and-oom-troubleshooting">Memory overhead limits and OOM troubleshooting</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">spark.executor.memoryOverhead reserves space for JVM overhead, off-heap allocations, and native libraries — undersizing it is a common cause of container-killed OOM errors.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-execution-lifecycle/#data-serialization-during-shuffle">Serialization format comparison (Java vs. Kryo)</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Java serialization is default but slow and verbose; Kryo is faster and more compact but requires registering custom classes for full benefit.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-performance-tuning/#shuffle-partition-sizing">Shuffle partition sizing</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">spark.sql.shuffle.partitions sets post-shuffle parallelism; too few creates huge partitions and OOMs, too many creates scheduling overhead from tiny tasks.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-performance-tuning/#data-skew-mitigation-and-salting-techniques">Data skew mitigation and salting techniques</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Adding a random salt to skewed join/group keys spreads them across more partitions, at the cost of an extra aggregation step to combine results.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-performance-tuning/#speculative-execution">Speculative execution</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Launches duplicate copies of unusually slow tasks on other executors and keeps whichever finishes first, mitigating stragglers from slow or failing nodes.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-performance-tuning/#task-concurrency-and-core-allocation-tuning">Task concurrency and core allocation tuning</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Balancing executor count, cores per executor, and memory per executor to maximize cluster utilization without oversubscribing CPU or triggering excessive GC.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-performance-tuning/#compression-codec-tradeoffs">Compression codec tradeoffs</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Shuffle and storage compression codecs (lz4, snappy, zstd) trade CPU cost for reduced I/O and network transfer volume differently under different workloads.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-performance-tuning/#network-and-shuffle-io-optimization">Network and shuffle I/O optimization</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">Tuning shuffle service settings and buffer sizes, and using the external shuffle service, to reduce network round-trips and avoid re-computing shuffle output on executor loss.</span></li>
          <li><span class="rm-index-sub-label"><a href="/level-3-technologies/batch-processing/spark-joins/#broadcast-hash-join">Broadcast join threshold tuning</a></span> <span class="rm-index-sub-desc">spark.sql.autoBroadcastJoinThreshold sets the max table size the optimizer will automatically broadcast; raising or disabling it changes which joins get broadcast versus shuffled.</span></li>
